+/*
+ * shepjit.c: SHEPHERD-VITA device kit #8 (vita/notes/kit8-jit.md) -- the baseline JIT tier of the Mono interpreter: entry points,
+ * the copy-and-patch trace compiler, the ForVM code block, batched publishing, monitoring ('compile only the loops that pay off'), the
+ * lockstep check and the reports. See shepjit.h for the model.
+ *
+ * THREADS. Interpreter threads only count entry-point hits and queue a trace (mono_shepjit_queue). ONE compiler thread (pinned like the
+ * host's latency-tolerant workers: the 4th core when it is open) compiles, and it alone ever calls sceKernelOpenVMDomain / writes the
+ * block / sceKernelSyncVMDomain / sceKernelCloseVMDomain -- the rules of the owner's console (devicelogs/20261021-kit7-owner-extras/
+ * jitprobe.txt: the thread that Opens must do the writes; Open is not nestable; code runs while the domain is open; Sync makes rewritten
+ * code visible to another core, 200 / 200). It publishes in BATCHES: everything it compiled since the last window goes into the block in
+ * one Open / copy / Sync / Close window (the console: an Open/Close pair ~11 us, write + Sync ~5.7 us per routine). Only then does an entry
+ * point switch to MINT_SJ_ENTER (after a barrier; the trace's fn pointer is valid at every moment, so a racing interpreter thread either
+ * runs the trace or keeps interpreting).
+ *
+ * FIDELITY. A trace runs the interpreter's own opcode bodies (shepjit-ops.h, the same compiler and flags), on the interpreter's frame, in
+ * the interpreter's order; it stops BEFORE any instruction it does not compile and before any check that would throw / initialise a class
+ * / poll a safepoint, and the interpreter runs that instruction itself. No GC can happen inside a trace (coop suspend: a trace never polls;
+ * a pending safepoint makes it leave). SHEPHERD_JIT=lockstep compiles no heap store and runs every trace, then -- on the same frame,
+ * restored -- the interpreter's walker (the interpreter's own opcode bodies), compares both (exit point + every byte of the frame) and
+ * keeps the interpreter's result.
+ */
+#include "config.h"
+#include <string.h>
